L["samaritan-woman"] = """Jesus and his disciples were traveling from Judea back to Galilee, and the shortest route went through Samaria. Many Jews avoided Samaria. The two peoples had a long history of hostility, and a Jewish teacher would not normally speak to a Samaritan, let alone a woman. But John says Jesus had to go through Samaria.

About noon, Jesus came to a town called Sychar and sat down by Jacob's well, tired from the journey. His disciples had gone into town to buy food. A Samaritan woman came to draw water. Most women came in the cool of the morning, so coming at noon suggests she may have wanted to avoid the others. Jesus asked her, will you give me a drink?

She was surprised. You are a Jew and I am a Samaritan woman. How can you ask me for a drink? Jesus answered, if you knew the gift of God and who it is that asks you for a drink, you would have asked him and he would have given you living water. She said, sir, you have nothing to draw with and the well is deep. Where can you get this living water? Jesus replied, everyone who drinks this water will be thirsty again, but whoever drinks the water I give them will never thirst. Indeed, the water I give them will become in them a spring of water welling up to eternal life.

She said, sir, give me this water so that I won't get thirsty and have to keep coming here to draw water. Then Jesus said something unexpected. Go, call your husband and come back. I have no husband, she replied. Jesus said, you are right. The fact is, you have had five husbands, and the man you now have is not your husband. He wasn't trying to shame her. He was showing her that he knew her whole story and still sat and talked with her.

She changed the subject to an old argument. Our ancestors worshiped on this mountain, but you Jews claim that the place where we must worship is in Jerusalem. Jesus said, a time is coming and has now come when the true worshipers will worship the Father in the Spirit and in truth. God is spirit, and his worshipers must worship in the Spirit and in truth. The place wasn't the point.

The woman said, I know that Messiah is coming. When he comes, he will explain everything to us. Then Jesus said, I, the one speaking to you, am he. It was the first time in John's gospel that Jesus plainly said he was the Messiah, and he said it to a Samaritan woman.

Just then his disciples returned and were surprised to find him talking with a woman. But no one asked why. Then, leaving her water jar, the woman went back to the town and said to the people, come, see a man who told me everything I ever did. Could this be the Messiah? They came out of the town and made their way toward him.

Why does this matter? Jesus crossed every barrier of race, gender and reputation to offer this woman new life. She left her water jar behind, because she had found something better. She became one of the first evangelists.

What happened after? Many of the Samaritans from that town believed in him because of her testimony. They asked him to stay with them, and he stayed two days. Many more became believers because of his words. They said to the woman, we no longer believe just because of what you said. Now we have heard for ourselves, and we know that this man really is the Savior of the world.

What does this mean for us today? Everyone has a thirst that nothing else can satisfy. Jesus meets us in our ordinary routines, knows everything about us, and offers living water anyway. It also shows that no one is too far outside to be welcomed. And it shows how to share faith. She simply told what had happened and invited people to come see for themselves.

That is why this story is so important. It says that Jesus is the Savior of the whole world, and that the water he gives satisfies for good."""
